{"kind":"request","text":"State the job gitseq does, and lead with the solo-developer-in-an-organization route. Hugh's judgement, 2026-08-10, in his words: I want to use multiple local agents with individual skills and capabilities, coordinating around a development project — and gitseq is a do-it-yourself way to get there. The documentation implies that job rather than stating it. README.md leads with a different and also true proposition, document currency: its Why section argues that every document is a cache nobody invalidates. Agents appear incidentally, as one item in a list of use cases at line 76. So a reader shopping for agent coordination has to infer that this is for them. Say it instead, near the top, in the reader's words, and let the cache argument follow as the reason the record has to be ordered and signed rather than a wiki. Second, the easiest onboarding is a developer running gs init on a local copy of a repository they already work in, with their own agents, and no expectation that the organization stands up a central sequencer; the full everyone-on-the-same-genesis model is functionally dependent on containerization, which is not planned yet. Yet every entry path starts from an empty directory: docs/how-to/end-to-end.md opens with mktemp -d, and the docs map sends a new reader there second. Nothing covers adopting gitseq alone inside an organization that has adopted nothing, which is the case almost every first reader is in, and that omission reads as a precondition, implying the organization must agree before you can begin. Put the solo route first, the shared-genesis route beside it, and say which one is supported today.","body":{"conditions":"Sequenced after the ownership-descent identity work lands, because that change rewrites the first-use provisioning story any onboarding page would describe. Then: README.md and docs/README.md state the job in the reader's words near the top, before the mechanism, and present two adoption routes side by side with solo-in-an-organization first and shared-genesis second, each saying plainly which one the current build supports and why. A how-to page covers the solo route starting from a repository the reader already works in rather than an empty directory, provisioning more than one agent identity with different roles, and its commands are runnable so the docset example gate executes them, thereby proving that gs init works in a repository that already has history and that two agents can hold distinct identities in it. The proposition is argued by naming checkable properties, not by claiming the field: no server or central service, one git ref, signed and ordered acts, attribution per agent, and an audit anyone can run from a clone. No comparative claim about other coordination tools appears, named or unnamed, because such a claim cannot be verified and ages badly. docs/getting-started.md keeps the public clone line the root public-surface test pins, every touched page keeps front matter with live rests_on anchors, and the docset surface, example, anchor and flare gates pass. Full gates and a clean merge-tree against current main; ready-for-review report offers the exact head.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}